// fleet domain -- the TRIAL CLOCK and IDLE HIBERNATION (epic memql#3852, task
// memql#3856).
//
// Five scheduled sweeps. Together they are the entire lifecycle of a customer
// who never pays: nudged at day 7 and day 12, suspended at day 14, offered
// their data back for 14 more, and then torn down.
//
// =============================================================================
//   Why these are sweeps at all, and how they are allowed to read
// =============================================================================
//
// Everything else in this domain reacts to a row that changed. A clock cannot:
// nothing writes a row when a date passes. So these five are `@trigger(schedule=)`
// and they READ -- which is the one thing this domain's automations otherwise
// never do, because an automation's actor is not the row's owner and a
// caller-scoped read from inside one returns nothing while looking correct.
//
// What makes it legal is the pair of annotations on the queries they call
// (queries.memql, "Internal sweeps"): `@serverOnly` refuses a client-originated
// call outright, and `@unbounded` declares that reading every row is the intent
// -- with the reason recorded, so the pagination gate does not silently truncate
// a sweep to its first page and leave the tail of the fleet unswept. That is the
// same shape `usersInDeletionCooldown` has carried in the identity domain, and
// deploy/fleet/authz_test.go checks BOTH annotations are present, because either
// alone is a defect wearing the other's clothes.
//
// =============================================================================
//   Every sweep reads exactly ONE concept, and that is why the clocks are
//   denormalised
// =============================================================================
//
// A sweep cannot join. So the clock a sweep reads has to be on the row it
// sweeps: `instance.trialEndsAt` is copied from the subscription, and
// `instance.suspendedAt` is the grace clock on the DATA rather than on the
// billing relationship.
//
// That is not duplication for convenience -- it is what makes the sweeps
// possible at all, and it is the same reason `notifyEmail`, `billingEmail`, the
// two spend ceilings and the shape fields are denormalised. The cost is that a
// clock moved on the subscription must be re-stamped on the instance; the
// benefit is that no sweep in this file can be silently returning nothing.
//
// =============================================================================
//   The windowing lives in the loop, not in the filter
// =============================================================================
//
// A filter cannot call `addDuration`, so "trialEndsAt is between 7 and 8 days
// away" is computed per row inside the `forEach`. The bracket is one day wide
// and the sweep fires daily, which is what makes each nudge fire EXACTLY ONCE
// per instance -- no duplicates from a wide bracket, no gaps from a narrow one.
// Identity's deletion reminders use the same construction for the same reason.
//
// =============================================================================
//   What a trial costs, and what bounds it
// =============================================================================
//
// A trial runs Graph-level FEATURES on Node-shaped infrastructure (the `solo`
// profile, `entry` database) with the tier's hard cap: 500 message credits,
// `overagePolicy: throttle`.
//
// The caps are enforced in the tenant's own process by the spend ceiling
// (task memql#3854), not by these sweeps. That distinction matters: a sweep runs
// once a day, and a runaway does not wait for it. What these do is bound the
// trial in TIME; what bounds it in SPEND is a limit on the other side of the
// boundary that needs no network and no clock.

use fleet.logic.{ runningInstances, suspendedInstances, trialingSubscriptions }
use fleet.mutations.{ endSubscription, requestInstanceSuspend, requestInstanceTeardown }
use platform.mutations.{ stageOutboundRequest }

// =============================================================================
//   Day 7 and day 12 -- the nudges
// =============================================================================

/// Daily 09:00 UTC: tell every trial that is a week from ending. The first of two nudges; the second
/// is two days out (below), which is when somebody who meant to buy actually acts.
///
/// The email is keyed on the instance AND the milestone, so the two nudges are different outbound
/// rows and neither suppresses the other -- `stageOutboundRequest` is idempotent by requestId, so a
/// shared key would mean the day-12 message silently never sent.
@trigger(schedule="0 0 9 * * *")
automation trialNudgeDay7 {
  step decide {
    logic runningInstances ( event )
  }
  step notify {
    forEach item in decide.result where item.payload.tier == "trial" && item.payload.trialEndsAt > addDuration(now, "P7D") && item.payload.trialEndsAt <= addDuration(now, "P8D") {
      stageOutboundRequest {
        requestId:   concat("fleet-trial-nudge-7-", item.id),
        medium:      "email",
        target:      item.payload.notifyEmail,
        subject:     "One week left on your MemQL Cloud trial",
        body:        concat("Your trial instance at https://", item.payload.domain, " has a week to run. Add a plan in Orbit to keep it -- nothing is lost if you decide later, but the instance pauses when the trial ends."),
        requestedBy: "fleet.trialNudgeDay7"
      }
    }
  }
}

/// Daily 09:00 UTC: the two-days-out nudge. Deliberately a different message from the day-7 one --
/// this is the last one before the instance pauses, and it says what happens next rather than
/// repeating the invitation.
@trigger(schedule="0 0 9 * * *")
automation trialNudgeDay12 {
  step decide {
    logic runningInstances ( event )
  }
  step notify {
    forEach item in decide.result where item.payload.tier == "trial" && item.payload.trialEndsAt > addDuration(now, "P2D") && item.payload.trialEndsAt <= addDuration(now, "P3D") {
      stageOutboundRequest {
        requestId:   concat("fleet-trial-nudge-12-", item.id),
        medium:      "email",
        target:      item.payload.notifyEmail,
        subject:     "Your MemQL Cloud trial ends in two days",
        body:        concat("Your trial instance at https://", item.payload.domain, " pauses in two days. Your data stays exactly where it is for 14 days after that, and adding a plan in Orbit brings it straight back."),
        requestedBy: "fleet.trialNudgeDay12"
      }
    }
  }
}

// =============================================================================
//   Day 14 -- the trial ends
// =============================================================================

/// Daily 02:00 UTC: pause every trial instance whose clock has run out. Scale-to-zero, data intact
/// -- which is the whole shape of the offer: a trial that ends does not destroy anything, it stops
/// costing us compute.
///
/// The instance and the subscription are closed by two separate sweeps, because neither can read the
/// other. They are eventually consistent with each other, and the order does not matter: a paused
/// instance whose subscription is still `trialing` for a few hours is a customer who cannot use a
/// service they are not paying for, which is the intended state either way.
@trigger(schedule="0 0 2 * * *")
automation trialExpirySuspend {
  step decide {
    logic runningInstances ( event )
  }
  step suspend {
    forEach item in decide.result where item.payload.tier == "trial" && exists(item.payload.trialEndsAt) && item.payload.trialEndsAt < now {
      requestInstanceSuspend {
        instanceId: item.id
      }
    }
  }
}

/// Daily 02:00 UTC: close out every subscription whose trial has ended, and start the 14-day clock
/// on the data.
///
/// `paused`, not `cancelled`. The customer did not cancel -- they ran out of trial, which is a
/// different fact about the same person and the one that decides whether "convert" or "resubscribe"
/// is the right word in every message that follows.
@trigger(schedule="0 0 2 * * *")
automation trialExpiryClose {
  step decide {
    logic trialingSubscriptions ( event )
  }
  step close {
    forEach item in decide.result where exists(item.payload.trialEndsAt) && item.payload.trialEndsAt < now {
      endSubscription {
        subscriptionId: item.id,
        status:         "paused",
        graceEndsAt:    addDuration(now, "P14D")
      }
    }
  }
}

// =============================================================================
//   Hibernation -- an instance nobody is using
// =============================================================================

/// Daily 03:00 UTC: scale to zero any instance that has served nothing for 14 days.
///
/// THIS APPLIES TO EVERY TIER, not just trials, and that is deliberate. A paying customer's idle
/// instance costs us exactly as much as an idle trial's, and resuming is a scale-up rather than a
/// restore -- the data never moved. What a paid tier buys is not "always warm", it is capacity when
/// they use it; a tier that genuinely needs zero cold starts is a product decision with a price
/// attached, not a default.
///
/// `lastActivityAt` is stamped by the TENANT's own heartbeat, because the control plane cannot see
/// the tenant's traffic. An instance that has never reported activity therefore has an empty field
/// and is skipped rather than hibernated -- fail-safe in the direction that costs money rather than
/// the one that surprises a customer.
@trigger(schedule="0 0 3 * * *")
automation hibernateIdleInstances {
  step decide {
    logic runningInstances ( event )
  }
  step suspend {
    forEach item in decide.result where exists(item.payload.lastActivityAt) && addDuration(item.payload.lastActivityAt, "P14D") < now {
      requestInstanceSuspend {
        instanceId: item.id
      }
    }
  }
}

// =============================================================================
//   The end of the grace period
// =============================================================================

/// Daily 04:00 UTC: tear down instances that have been suspended for 14 days.
///
/// This is the only automation in the fleet that destroys data, and it is a SWEEP rather than a
/// reaction for exactly that reason: nothing a customer or an operator does in one action reaches
/// it. The path is pause, then fourteen days, then this.
///
/// The capability script takes a final backup before deleting anything and ABORTS if that backup
/// fails, so a teardown that could not preserve the data does not happen. The instance is left in
/// `tearing_down` in that case -- a state that says what was being attempted, which is what an
/// operator needs and what a row that looked finished would deny them.
///
/// `suspendedAt` is the clock, and it is the instance's OWN field rather than the subscription's
/// `graceEndsAt`. The two normally agree; where they do not, this one is the honest one -- it
/// measures how long the data has actually been cold, which is the thing the grace period is about.
@trigger(schedule="0 0 4 * * *")
automation teardownAfterGrace {
  step decide {
    logic suspendedInstances ( event )
  }
  step destroy {
    forEach item in decide.result where exists(item.payload.suspendedAt) && addDuration(item.payload.suspendedAt, "P14D") < now {
      requestInstanceTeardown {
        instanceId: item.id
      }
    }
  }
}
